the one thing you want people to get
across.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.080
The one thing you want people to remember.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.440
Again, the Apple website, they do this all
the time.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.030
It takes courage to be simple.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.450
It takes courage to communicate simply.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:29.380
If you cannot communicate what you do in
ten words

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:34.470
or less, a short sentence, or say, 140
characters, go back to the drawing board.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.080
Once you give me the big picture, as an
audience member,

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.470
I need to understand the problem that
you're trying to solve.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.080
I call this introducing the antagonist.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.702
Because every great story, and a
presentation is a

00:14:50.702 --> 00:14:54.927
story, every great story requires a hero,
and a villain.

00:14:54.927 --> 00:14:57.395
So think of your presentation the same
way.

00:14:57.395 --> 00:15:01.180
In 1984, when Steve Jobs first introduced
Macintosh.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:04.196
Macintosh obviously, the Mac was the hero.

00:15:04.196 --> 00:15:06.930
IBM was the villain.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.310
At least in the Steve Jobs narrative.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:13.190
So he actually crafted the story.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:17.620
IBM would play the villain part of the
role.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.500
Mac would come in to save the day.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:23.010
IBM was a mainframe computer, at the time.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:26.980
Mainframe computer maker, just getting in
the personal computers for the first time.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:30.240
And Steve Jobs created this, this
presentation of

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:34.160
messaging around, Apple would be the only
one

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.820
to stand in, in IBM's way and make

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:40.060
the world safer, us creative people in the
world.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:44.760
It was, it was very dramatic stuff, but he
actually crafted the narrative.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:51.500
But more often than not, the enemy in a
Steve Jobs presentation is not a

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:54.320
competitor, or one competitor, it's, could
be a

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:58.010
category of problems in need of a
solution.

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:02.770
So, when Steve Jobs introduced the iPhone
in 2007, he didn't just point to one

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:08.760
villain, but a problem among all the
villains, in need of a solution.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:15.165
Watch as he outlines the problem, and
offers a solution, all in two minutes.

00:16:15.165 --> 00:16:18.255
&gt;&gt; Why do we revolutionary user interface?

00:16:18.255 --> 00:16:19.390
I mean.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.730
Here is four smartphones.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.660
Right, the Moto Q, Blackberry Palm Treo,
Nokia E62.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.760
The usual suspects.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.170
And, what's wrong with their user
interface?

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:33.950
Well, the problem with them is really sort
of in the bottom 40 there.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.620
It's, it's this stuff right here.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.533
They all have these keyboards, they're
there whether

00:16:39.533 --> 00:16:41.720
or not you need them to be there.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.280
And they all have these control buttons
that are

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.120
fixed in plastic and are the same for
every application.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:51.649
Well, every application wants a slightly
different user interface,

00:16:51.649 --> 00:16:54.188
a slightly optimized set of buttons, just
for it.

00:16:54.188 --> 00:16:58.080
And what happens if you think of a great
idea six months from now?

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.120
You can't run around and add a button to
these things.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:01.470
They're already shipped.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.060
So, what do you do?

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:07.710
It doesn't work because the buttons and
controls can't change.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:12.160
They can't change for each application,
and they can't change down the road

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:14.720
if you think of another great idea you
want to add to this product.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.560
Well, how do you solve this?

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:20.480
Hm, it turns out, we have solved it.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.050
We solved it in computers twenty years
ago.

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:28.730
We saw that the big Mac screen they could
display anything we want.

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:31.630
Put any user interface up.

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.039
And a pointing device.

00:17:33.039 --> 00:17:35.170
We solved it with the mouse.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:40.490
We solved this problem, so how are we
going to take this to a mobile device?

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:43.882
What we're going to do is get rid of all
these buttons.

00:17:43.882 --> 00:17:46.355
We'll just make a giant screen.

00:17:46.355 --> 00:17:48.553
A giant screen.

00:17:48.553 --> 00:17:51.260
Now, how are we going to communicate with
this?

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.993
We don't want to carry it around a mouse,
right?

00:17:53.993 --> 00:17:55.530
So what are we going to do?

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.000
A stylus, right?

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:57.750
A stylus.

00:17:58.940 --> 00:17:59.080
No.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:17:59.580
Who

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:02.719
wants a stylus?

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:06.486
You have to hit 'em and put 'em away, and
you lose them, yuck!

00:18:06.486 --> 00:18:09.389
Nobody wants a stylus, so let's not use a
stylus.

00:18:09.389 --> 00:18:13.657
We're gonna use the best pointing device
in the wold, we're gonna use the

00:18:13.657 --> 00:18:19.540
pointing device that we're all born with,
with one tab, we're gonna use our fingers.

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:21.470
We're gonna touch this with our fingers.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:25.361
And, we have invented a new technology
called

00:18:25.361 --> 00:18:30.790
multi-touch which is phenomenal, it works
like magic [LAUGH].

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:32.574
You don't need a stylus.

00:18:32.574 --> 00:18:37.886
It's far more accurate than any touch
display that's ever been shipped.

00:18:37.886 --> 00:18:41.676
It ignores unintended touches, it's super
smart,

00:18:41.676 --> 00:18:44.473
you can do multi finger gestures on it.

00:18:44.473 --> 00:18:51.135
And boy, have we had with it!

00:18:51.135 --> 00:18:54.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] All right.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:55.560
I'm glad it stopped there.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.890
Take a view of that slide.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:18:59.500
We're gonna get back to something like
that.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.390
What do you notice about those slides, by
the way?

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:05.230
Simple, visual.

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.480
Do you notice what he did?

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:07.310
He did three things.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:12.250
He informed, he educated, and he had fun
at the same time.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:13.250
Information.

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:15.420
Education, and entertainment.

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:16.630
All in two minutes.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:18.340
I find that quite extraordinary.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:20.920
Very few communicators have that skill.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:25.670
But you need to begin by asking yourself,
what problem do I solve?

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:26.670
What problem do I solve?

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:29.910
What's the villain here?

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.210
And then you can offer the solution.

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:33.810
Enter the hero.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:36.770
The solution better sell a benefit,
though.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:38.670
What's the benefit behind it?

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:41.280
People want to know, what's in it for me?

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:42.830
I learned this in journalism 101.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:45.510
I went to UCLA.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:49.020
Went to Northwestern to study journalism,
then I went

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.680
to CNN and some other media outlets after
that.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:55.130
But I learned this my first day of
journalism school.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:56.150
What's in it for me?

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:57.660
Why do I care?

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:03.620
Let me show you an example of how we sell
the benefit in some of my clients.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:09.130
At CES this year, Intel launched a new
micro

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:12.360
processor, it's called Sandy Bridge,
that's the code name.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.260
Sandy Bridge actually, is the largest,

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.190
biggest technological leap in Intel's
history.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:21.700
It's a big deal for Intel, and it's a big
deal for consumers.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:24.460
Here's the technical definition.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:30.900
Sandy Bridge is based on the 32 manometer
manufacturing process.

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:34.670
It's processing cores feature hyper
threading and turbo boost technology.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.510
Are any of you inspired and excited about
running

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.340
out today to buy one of these new
computers?

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:42.300
Yeah.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.240
Good, he gets it.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:45.820
That's powerful stuff though.

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:50.630
That's actually technology that's going to
improve your life significantly.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.420
But I don't see too many hands and
nobody's interested yet.

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:56.660
Okay, let's try this.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:21:00.153
What if you walked into a Best Buy and
somebody said something

00:21:00.153 --> 00:21:03.869
like think of the micro processor as the
brain of your computer.

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:09.230
Now with these Intel chips you get two
brains in one computer.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:12.200
It's the fastest chip on the market.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.130
What does that mean to you?

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:17.690
Video games, will look amazingly
realistic.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:21.550
You'll be able to transfer video and
upload it to YouTube much more quickly.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:25.520
In fact, what took four minutes to encode
will now take 30 seconds.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:28.950
And finally, it's much more energy
efficient.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.240
That means you'll get much longer battery
life.

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:35.772
So the next time any of you are looking
for a new

00:21:35.772 --> 00:21:40.699
computer, do you think you're gonna ask
for this new generation Intel processor?

00:21:40.699 --> 00:21:42.560
Do you think you'll want it now?

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:45.420
Yes.

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:46.070
Yeah.

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:46.990
More hands.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.140
Why?

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:53.810
I just, I, what I told you earlier is
exactly the same thing.

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:59.780
It was exactly the same thing, but I
changed the messaging.

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:02.310
Instead of focusing on the chip and its

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:05.670
features, what would the features do for
you.

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:09.220
Sell the benefit.

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:14.910
Introduce the hero which is your product,
your service, your company, but you

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:18.680
better tell me why I need it and how it
will improve my life.

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:22.782
What was the big question when Steve Jobs
introduced the iPad?

00:22:22.782 --> 00:22:24.600
A lot of people were skeptical.

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:27.600
They said, they asked themselves well why
do I need another device?

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.130
I have a smart phone and I have a laptop.

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:32.980
What's in it for me?

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:34.550
Steve Jobs gave us the answer.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:37.720
&gt;&gt; There's laptops.

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.330
And smart phones now.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:43.230
Everybody uses a laptop in the work
circle,

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:49.810
and the question has arisen lately is
there room

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:53.230
for a third category of device in the
middle?

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:57.800
Something that's between a laptop and a
smartphone.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:00.469
And of course we've pondered this question
for years as well.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:07.163
The bar is pretty high and in order to
really create a new category of devices,

00:23:07.163 --> 00:23:12.800
those devices are gonna have to be far
better at doing some key tasks.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.452
In order to be far better at doing some
really

00:23:15.452 --> 00:23:19.480
important things better than a laptop,
better than a cell phone.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:22.180
What kind of tests?

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.250
Well, things like browsing the Web.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:27.865
Now, that's a pretty tall order.

00:23:27.865 --> 00:23:31.505
Something's better at browsing the Web
than a laptop?

00:23:31.505 --> 00:23:32.864
Okay.

00:23:32.864 --> 00:23:34.000
Getting email.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.098
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:23:36.098 --> 00:23:37.899
Enjoying and sharing photographs.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:41.950
Watching videos.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:46.000
Enjoy your music collection.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:48.260
Playing games.

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:50.200
Reading Ebooks.

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:55.084
If there is going to be a third category
device it is going to have

00:23:55.084 --> 00:24:00.300
to be better at these kinds of tasks than
a lap top or a smart phone.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.100
Otherwise it has no reason for being.

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:08.000
Now, some people have thought Apple's
Netbook.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:13.097
The problem is, Netbooks aren't better at
anything.

00:24:13.097 --> 00:24:18.965
[LAUGH] They, they're slow, they have low
quality

00:24:18.965 --> 00:24:24.838
displays, and they run frumpy, old PC
software.

00:24:24.838 --> 00:24:29.326
So they're not better than a lap top at
anything, they're just cheaper.

00:24:29.326 --> 00:24:36.968
They're just cheap lap tops and we don't
think iii.

00:24:36.968 --> 00:24:40.336
But we think we've got something that is.

00:24:40.336 --> 00:24:44.385
And we'd like to show to you for the first
time.

00:24:44.385 --> 00:24:50.550
And we call it the iPad.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:57.640
So, let me show it to you now.

00:24:58.990 --> 00:24:59.950
This is what it looks like.

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:02.260
I

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:04.630
actually

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:09.360
have one right

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:11.930
here.

00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:17.795
&gt;&gt; He always just happens to have one
right here.

00:25:17.795 --> 00:25:22.380
[LAUGH] He informs, he educates, and he
entertains.

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:26.600
When's the last time you laughed at a
presentation or had fun with it?

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:29.110
That takes courage, but it also takes
thought.

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:32.510
You need to answer the question before you

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:36.450
even open your slides, why should my
audience care?

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:39.220
Because that's the only question on their
minds.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:43.190
They don't really care about your
technology or your features.

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:45.530
Or the commission that you need to make.

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:46.980
They don't care about any of that.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:48.430
They just wanna know what's in it for me.

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:50.040
Why should I care?

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:51.450
Answer that.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:52.460
Don't leave them guessing.

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:56.260
Now I know many of you, at least the first

00:25:56.260 --> 00:26:00.640
years, unless I'm mistaken, are taking a
lot of quantitative courses.

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:02.100
That true?

00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:04.730
Yup, a lot of heavy financial courses this
year.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:06.400
Let's talk about something then.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:09.300
Bringing numbers to life.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:15.830
Steve Jobs will rarely introduce a
datapoint, a statistic

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:20.449
without putting it into some kind of
context that people can understand.

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:26.285
So for example in 2001 when Steve Jobs
introduced the iPod

00:26:26.285 --> 00:26:30.690
for the first time he said it had five
gigabytes of storage.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:33.510
Five gigabytes of storage.

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:35.526
I mean I know what is it today many of you

00:26:35.526 --> 00:26:40.380
might have purchased the latest one, 160
gigs or something like that?

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:42.425
But it was five gigs in 2001.

00:26:42.425 --> 00:26:44.420
What does that mean to anybody?

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:47.150
Five gigabytes of storage, okay?

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:50.380
That's not that interesting, I'm not even
sure what it means.

00:26:50.380 --> 00:26:52.870
Oh, it meant 1,000 songs.

00:26:52.870 --> 00:26:55.810
Storage capacity for 1,000 songs.

00:26:55.810 --> 00:27:00.220
Now it's more interesting, but Steve Jobs
goes one step further.

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:03.870
It's 1,000 songs in your pocket.

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:05.320
Now I'm in interested.

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:07.050
Now I'm inspired.

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:09.390
Steve Jobs does this all the time.

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.410
As do all the other executives at Apple.

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:14.390
I've seen many other presentations as
well.

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:16.980
It's very affective.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:21.841
Don't just throw out a big number without
putting it into

00:27:21.841 --> 00:27:26.980
some kind of context that is relatable to
me and my life.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:29.380
Cisco has the same type of challenge.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:35.430
Cisco makes big routers and switches that
nobody ever sees so they've become

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:37.470
very good at putting big numbers

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:39.780
into perspective and making them very
interesting.

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:44.590
Last year, Cisco released a CRS3 router.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:48.320
Capable of handling 322 terabytes per
second.

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:51.250
It's a big number isn't it.

00:27:51.250 --> 00:27:54.250
Not very interesting, just sounds like a
big number.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:58.445
So, when John Chambers, the CEO, was
giving presentations on

00:27:58.445 --> 00:28:02.640
it, in every presentation, and in every
interview, he rarely

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:06.058
even used the number 322 terabits, but he
did say

00:28:06.058 --> 00:28:10.360
powerful enough to stream every movie ever
made in four minutes.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:16.130
Powerful enough to download the entire
Library of Congress in one second.

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:19.580
That's interesting.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:22.690
That actually got picked up by a lot of
mainstream

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:27.620
press who otherwise would have no business
covering a Cisco router.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:29.300
But he made it interesting.

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:33.240
Put numbers into perspective.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.290
Bring numbers to life.

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:38.250
I just want to introduce that concept for
you because I know a

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:42.219
lot of you are taking those sort of, those
classes in your first year.

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:45.560
What have I been doing in the last few
slides?

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:50.730
I've been trying to keep them as simple as
possible and as visual as possible.

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:55.800
When you are creating slides it helps to
think visually.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.425
How many of you give PowerPoints, create

00:28:58.425 --> 00:29:01.430
PowerPoints, how many of you use
PowerPoint?

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:03.970
Everybody.

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:06.220
How many of you use Apple Keynote?

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:08.070
Okay.

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:08.820
A few of you.

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:09.980
It's a beautiful program.

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:11.940
Very refined program.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.630
All my clients use PowerPoint.

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:15.970
97% of us use PowerPoint so I tend

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:18.930
to use PowerPoint as well for
compatibility reasons.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.120
But this goes beyond PowerPoint.

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:25.330
I have seen really awful keynote
presentations as

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:27.090
well I, so I don't think it's some

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:30.990
much the presentation software as much as
it

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:34.630
is how to tell the story using the
software.

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:40.008
I think what happens is Microsoft and
PowerPoint

00:29:40.008 --> 00:29:42.675
sort of make it easy to be mediocre.

00:29:42.675 --> 00:29:44.233
This is what it does.

00:29:44.233 --> 00:29:48.624
It forces you to create a title slide and
then add bullets

00:29:48.624 --> 00:29:53.865
and more bullets and sub bullets until you
are really in the weeds.

00:29:53.865 --> 00:29:55.862
[LAUGH] And oh, there, there's empty
space.

00:29:55.862 --> 00:29:57.071
We can't have that.

00:29:57.071 --> 00:30:00.166
So, let me add some cheesy clip art.

00:30:00.166 --> 00:30:06.190
And, you know I'm an MBA, so I need to add
a chart and a squiggly

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:12.558
line, and there you have it the worlds
ugliest slide at least, so I

00:30:12.558 --> 00:30:18.905
thought when I first created that slide,
until I saw this [LAUGH].

00:30:18.905 --> 00:30:21.410
That's a real slide.

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:28.900
This was delivered in the US, among the US
military commanders.

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:32.160
And one general actually said, he actually
said this, if

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.787
I can understand this slide, we'll have
won the war.

00:30:34.787 --> 00:30:37.278
[LAUGH] So, I thought I could make a
really

00:30:37.278 --> 00:30:41.070
bad PowerPoint, when I wanted to, but this
stops it.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:42.800
There's no way I can compete.

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:45.260
That is a bad Powerpoint slide.

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:47.300
What's the difference?

00:30:47.300 --> 00:30:48.630
What's the difference?

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:51.350
The difference is that a slide in a

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:56.980
Steve Jobs presentation simply complements
the messenger, its Steve

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:59.710
Jobs telling the story, Steve Jobs is the

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:02.640
narrator, think of the sort of a broadway
play.

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:04.400
Because he is very theatrical.

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:07.990
Steve Jobs is the central figure.

00:31:07.990 --> 00:31:09.270
The narrator.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:10.780
The slides are in the back drop.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:11.310
That's all they do.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.180
It serve to compliment the story.

00:31:15.290 --> 00:31:19.200
The average PowerPoint slide has 40 words.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:26.650
It is difficult to find 40 words in ten
slides in a Steve Jobs presentations.

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:30.570
You will get words and text and images as
well.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:34.470
There's a reason for this whether he's
doing this consciously

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:36.820
or not neuroscientists will tell

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:40.860
you there's something called picture
superiority.

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:44.100
It simply means that when information is
delivered.

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:48.660
Verbally, people will remember about 10%
of the information.

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:53.030
Add a picture or an image, retention goes
up to 65%.

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:58.112
In fact, too many words on slide is
actually very difficult for the

00:31:58.112 --> 00:32:03.680
brain to process because, according to
John Medina who I talk to.

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:07.833
The brain interprets every letter as a
picture, so

00:32:07.833 --> 00:32:12.380
what happens is the brain is literally
choking on text.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:16.360
Now, some of you, the more thoughtful
ones, the

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:19.600
smart MBAs are thinking to themselves well
I can read.

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:21.970
It doesn't bother me when I'm reading.

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:26.010
Yeah now try reading, and have somebody
else talk to you at the same time.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:27.520
Can you process both?

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:28.220
No.

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:32.390
So, why do we expect people to do so when
we're giving PowerPoint presentations?

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:35.880
Let's create wordy slides with a hundred
words on them, and I'm gonna tell

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:37.750
you something really complicated, and I'm
gonna

00:32:37.750 --> 00:32:39.920
expect you to concentrate on either one.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:40.500
It doesn't work.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:42.630
What does Steve Jobs do?

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:47.080
Let me create, let me create a really ugly
slide around the Macbook Air.

00:32:48.190 --> 00:32:51.869
This, I think, would have been a typical
slide, but,

00:32:51.869 --> 00:32:56.820
again, after looking at that other
PowerPoint, this looks like genius.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:01.340
Those of you who are looking at

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:04.040
this closely can tell there's different
fonts sizes,

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:06.430
different sizes of shapes, there a little
clip

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:09.700
art, because, God forbid we have empty
space.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.740
This gives you all of the details about
the Macbook Air.

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:15.890
Well when Steve Jobs and his team were
trying

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:18.730
to decide, how do we communicate the
vision behind

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:21.770
this computer in a way that everybody's
gonna remember,

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:23.820
and how do we do that in the slide?

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:24.830
They came up with this.

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:31.569
It's so thin, it fits inside one of those
envelopes.

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:35.110
Why do we need any text on that?

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:36.530
What's more interesting?

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:37.870
What's more memorable?

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:40.020
This or that?

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:41.260
But this takes thought.

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:43.280
See this doesn't take a lot of thought.

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:45.410
This you just throw a bunch of words on
the slide.

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:50.290
That takes, practice, thought, research
ahead of time.

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:52.310
So think, visually.

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:55.340
And in order to think visually guess what,
you've

00:33:55.340 --> 00:34:00.730
got to start like this, sketching, brain
storming, white boarding.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:03.310
Before you open up the slides.

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:10.830
Visual slides help in creating what's
called a holy smokes moment.

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:16.060
This is that one moment in a presentation
that everybody's going to remember.

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:20.870
Everybody remembers when Steve Jobs pulled
the Macbook Air, out of the envelope.

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:23.050
At least everybody who is was in the
audience that day.

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:26.390
John Medina taught me, he said Carmine,
the

00:34:26.390 --> 00:34:29.565
brain does not pay attention, to boring
things.

00:34:29.565 --> 00:34:32.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So don't make it boring.

00:34:33.700 --> 00:34:37.570
When the brain detects, an emotionally
charged event.

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:41.090
Anger, fear, surprise.

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:44.100
It actually releases DOPAMINE in the
system, acting

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:48.650
as a mental post-it note, saying remember
this.

00:34:48.650 --> 00:34:51.960
Create that one moment of surprise.

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:54.740
Steve Jobs did, so when he introduced the
iPhone.

00:34:54.740 --> 00:34:56.230
He could have come out.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:34:59.900
And said hey, we're really excited today
to introduce this new technology.

00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:01.670
It's Apple's new smartphone.

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:05.390
First time, Apple has created a phone, I
can't wait to tell you about it.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.040
Yeah, he could have done that.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:08.210
Most people would have.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.208
Instead, Steve Jobs did this.

00:35:10.208 --> 00:35:15.263
&gt;&gt; Today, we're introducing.

00:35:15.263 --> 00:35:19.947
Three revolutionary products of this
class.

00:35:19.947 --> 00:35:29.204
[SOUND] The first one is a wide screen
iPod with touch controls.

00:35:29.204 --> 00:35:34.435
[SOUND] The second

00:35:34.435 --> 00:35:40.413
is a revolutionary

00:35:40.413 --> 00:35:45.275
mobile phone.

00:35:45.275 --> 00:35:53.034
[SOUND] And the third, is a breakthrough

00:35:53.034 --> 00:36:00.095
internet communications device.

00:36:00.095 --> 00:36:04.609
[SOUND].

00:36:04.609 --> 00:36:08.170
So three things.

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:11.030
A wide-screened iPod with touch controls,
a

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:15.766
revolutionary old phone, and a
breakthrough internet communications.

00:36:15.766 --> 00:36:24.519
An iPod, [COUGH] a phone, [LAUGH] an
internet communicator.

00:36:24.519 --> 00:36:27.940
An iPod, the phone.

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:33.569
Are you kidding me?

00:36:33.569 --> 00:36:40.615
These are not three separate devices.

00:36:40.615 --> 00:36:43.298
This is one device.

00:36:43.298 --> 00:36:50.348
[NOISE] And we're calling it iPhone.

00:36:50.348 --> 00:36:58.360
[INAUDIBLE] Today, today Apple is going to
reinvent the phone.

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:01.690
&gt;&gt; Pretty entertaining, isn't it?

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:05.460
What's the Twitter friendly headline from
that presentation?

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:10.280
Today Apple reinvents the phone.

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:13.370
That was on their press release, on their
website, and in the presentation.

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:15.319
What's the one thing you want me to
remember?

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:22.766
When you create visual presentations or
you think about how to move away from

00:37:22.766 --> 00:37:25.090
the slides in order to create these

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:28.296
emotionally charged events, it helps to
think

00:37:28.296 --> 00:37:33.183
about it this way what's a multi sensory
experience I can create that has

00:37:33.183 --> 00:37:35.907
absolutely nothing to do with the slides

00:37:35.907 --> 00:37:40.240
sometimes the most memorable parts of a
presentation.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:43.931
Are not about what's on the slide, and yet
we spend 98%

00:37:43.931 --> 00:37:48.720
of our time getting the fonts just right
and the right images.

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:53.490
Often times we gotta think about how to
connect with people beyond the slide.

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:58.110
Bill Gates has been doing a very good job
of this.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:01.180
Bill Gates is now the world's largest
philanthropist,

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:04.150
and he has the challenge of talking about

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:08.630
global problems, very complicated
problems, in simple to

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:12.330
understand language, which has been doing
very, very well.

00:38:13.400 --> 00:38:17.370
Last year he gave a talk about reducing
childhood deaths in Malaria.

00:38:18.830 --> 00:38:22.640
The most memorable part of his
presentation, one that

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:25.260
went viral, had nothing to do with his
slides.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:29.100
Although the slides were beautifully
created, they were very visual.

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:29.930
A lot of images.

00:38:29.930 --> 00:38:31.850
Very powerful slides.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:36.220
But the most memorable part of his
presentation was multi sensory.

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:37.630
It went beyond the slides.

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:42.160
&gt;&gt; For example, there's more money put

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:46.098
into baldness drugs than are put into
malaria.

00:38:46.098 --> 00:38:51.314
Now baldness is a terrible thing [LAUGH]
and rich

00:38:51.314 --> 00:38:56.782
men are afflicted [LAUGH] and so that's
why that.

00:38:56.782 --> 00:38:58.984
[COUGH] priorities.

00:38:58.984 --> 00:39:05.946
[INAUDIBLE] 18 million deaths a year
caused by Malaria.

00:39:05.946 --> 00:39:07.977
Greatly understating its impact.

00:39:07.977 --> 00:39:15.300
Over 200 million people [INAUDIBLE] can't
get the economies

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:22.220
of this areas going, because it holds
things back so much.

00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:27.432
Now malaria is transmitted by mosquito's I
brought some

00:39:27.432 --> 00:39:33.005
[INAUDIBLE] let them roam around the
auditorium a little bit.

00:39:33.005 --> 00:39:35.230
[LAUGH].

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:38.683
There's no reason only poor people should
have malaria.

00:39:38.683 --> 00:39:45.768
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:39:45.768 --> 00:39:46.410
&gt;&gt; But those mosquitoes are not.

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:52.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, he was going to say, those
mosquitoes are not infected.

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:55.420
I'll say, that is astonishing.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:57.660
He created a multi-sensory experience.

00:39:57.660 --> 00:40:00.850
It was a memorable experience, and he got
people to laugh

00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:04.770
about a serious subject like malaria, but
he made it memorable.

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:08.180
And I know as a fact that he's always
thinking, about how

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:13.100
to communicate these very difficult issues
in a way that people can understand.

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:18.330
Now there's one other topic I just want to
introduce to you briefly.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:19.600
I'm not gonna go into it.

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:23.160
We could do a whole half day workshop on
this, but we're not going to today.

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:25.430
I just want to introduce it to you because
it's important.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:27.990
You can have all these things.

00:40:27.990 --> 00:40:30.760
You create a great PowerPoint
presentation, great

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:33.300
messaging, you've got a hero and a
villain.

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:34.920
But you have it to deliver well.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:36.850
You have to deliver effectively.

00:40:36.850 --> 00:40:39.610
This is called mastering stage presence.

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:43.390
Great communicators all have great
presence.

00:40:43.390 --> 00:40:49.050
This is an important statistic, 65%, 65%
of the impression that

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:52.630
you leave on someone has little to do with
your message.

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:55.080
It has to do with your facial expressions.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:57.460
Your verbal delivery, your body language.

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:03.940
There are three things that you can do
today, that will help

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:08.080
you stand out from the vast majority of
public speakers and communicators.

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:09.960
Number one, eye contact.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.700
Make eye contact 80, 90% of the time.

00:41:12.700 --> 00:41:17.030
That's why I don't like it when people
have too many notes to read from.

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:20.980
Where if you put too many words on the
slide, you're breaking eye contact.

00:41:20.980 --> 00:41:23.410
Steve Jobs rarely breaks eye contact.

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:27.920
He will turn to a display, bring something
up, and turn back to the audience.

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:34.200
Open posture, open simply means there's
nothing in between me and you.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:38.330
If I had delivered this presentation
exactly the same way, but I had done

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:42.750
so like this, the whole time, would that
have left a different impression on you?

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:43.990
Why?

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:45.630
It's the same content?

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:49.890
Because 65% of the impression I'm leaving
on you, has little to do with the content.

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:53.060
And also hand gestures.

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:54.940
Use hand gestures.

00:41:54.940 --> 00:41:59.050
I'm Italian, so it's easy for me to use
hand gestures.

00:41:59.050 --> 00:42:00.350
But it's okay.

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:05.950
Researchers are finding that complex
thinkers use complex gestures.

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:07.350
A lot of people will ask me what do I

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:09.850
do with my hands, do I keep them in my
pocket?

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:11.890
Take them out, be animated.

00:42:11.890 --> 00:42:14.120
Be animated in voice and in body.

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:18.630
And finally, let's wrap all this together.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:23.190
Never forget that you are selling dreams,
not

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:29.320
products, because your customers do not
care about your company.

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:31.030
They don't care about your product or

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:33.480
your serviced, but they do care about
themselves.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:37.850
Their hopes, their goals, their dreams,
their ambitions.

00:42:37.850 --> 00:42:41.140
Help them achieve their dreams and you'll
inspire them.

00:42:41.140 --> 00:42:42.540
You'll win them over.

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:46.590
Steve Jobs has always been in the business
of selling dreams.

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:50.751
When he first got together with Wozniak in

00:42:50.751 --> 00:42:54.621
the spare bedroom of his parents house in
1974.

00:42:54.621 --> 00:42:56.941
That's actually where it started, not the

00:42:56.941 --> 00:42:59.870
garage, they were just playing with
electronics.

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:01.300
And Steve Jobs had a vision.

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:06.730
He said, I would love to make computers
that are easy to use for everyday people.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:10.850
He was always selling dreams.

00:43:10.850 --> 00:43:12.390
In 1997, remember that I told you that he
had

00:43:12.390 --> 00:43:16.320
returned to Apple, after being away for
about 12 years.

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:17.650
He returned to Apple.

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:21.170
In his first major presentation the
following year, he

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:26.400
paused at the end of his presentation and
reminded people.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:29.120
Of what Apple stood for as a brand.

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:30.230
It's very powerful moment.

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:35.040
&gt;&gt; I want to talk just a little bit about
Apple and the brand and I

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:40.640
want to use I think to livest.

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:42.410
You know, I think it always has to

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:44.590
be a little different, to buy an Apple
computer.

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:50.270
When we shipped the Ap 2, you had to think
different about computers.

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:52.650
Computers were these things you saw in
movies.

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:55.190
They occupied giant rooms.

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:57.530
They weren't an extension on your desktop.

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:00.260
You had to think differently, because

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:03.880
there wasn't any software at the
beginning.

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:06.809
You had to think differently once the
first computer arrived at school.

00:44:08.110 --> 00:44:12.150
What I've never been on before is an Apple
two.

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:14.330
I think [INAUDIBLE] when you bought a Mac.

00:44:14.330 --> 00:44:17.560
It was a totally different computer,
worked a totally

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:20.530
different way used a totally different
part of your brain.

00:44:20.530 --> 00:44:25.940
And it opened up a computer world for a
lot of people who thought differently.

00:44:25.940 --> 00:44:27.595
You were buying a computer [INAUDIBLE]
one.

00:44:27.595 --> 00:44:34.000
Now I think you still have to think of
[UNKNOWN].

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:39.347
And I think that people do [UNKNOWN] do
think

00:44:39.347 --> 00:44:43.490
that way and they are the [UNKNOWN] in
this world.

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:44.659
They are the people that are not just out

